### Agent summary

Common understanding was confirmed and implementation began. The canonical chezmoi source, active targets, VCS wrapper, tests, and applicable skills were inspected. The source repository was clean and one commit ahead of `origin/main`; remote standard notes were absent.

The first wrapper draft added existing-note replacement, then targeted tests passed. The draft was frozen for independent review and forward tests. `mise run check` initially failed before checking the changes because sandbox network policy blocked `starship.rs`; investigation classified this as an execution-environment constraint and the target-equivalent rerun passed.

Independent review found that a force replacement could lose concurrent updates and that snapshot did not expose whether a local notes ref existed. The implementation changed to compare-and-swap updates and added `local_notes_status`. Forward testing exposed cycle-boundary, redaction precedence, semantic-conflict, partial-push, Git-unavailable, and long-argv ambiguities; guidance and the wrapper were revised.

Further delta review showed that separate content/object reads could still race, Git stripspace changed full-message bytes, and note-blocked cycle closure could make a missing note permanent. The final wrapper reads content and object from one immutable blob, updates via an atomic notes-ref CAS, accepts `-F FILE`, disables stripspace, and tests concurrent rejection plus byte equality. The final wording keeps a task cycle open whenever its mandatory note remains unsaved, even alongside other blockers.

Final verification succeeded: shell parsing, `mise run test:vcs`, full `mise run check`, targeted rendered chezmoi diff, explicit application of four active targets, empty post-apply diffs, independent delta review, and all forward scenarios. Commit `778fc82` was created with the five source/test files only. No code or notes ref was pushed.

## Additional high-cost evidence

The review evolution above is retained because it records why a simple force-update interface is insufficient: preserving existing notes requires a single immutable read plus an atomic compare-and-swap of the notes ref, and byte-faithful transcripts require `--no-stripspace`. These concurrency and fidelity conditions are not obvious from the final policy text alone.
